            "snippets": [
              "# Is the USA winning or Losing the trade war?\n## lcy0x1\n### javyreed\nIf based on who is losing more, China is losing a lot more. US has much more leverage compared to China. Now with the weakened Yuan, China's debt crisis becomes even more of a problem, as most of the debt comes from USD. Also, a weakened economy puts a lot of pressure on the rich, where it incentifies the wealthy chinese to bring money outside of China even more",
              "### Savage_X\nCounterpoint - a weakened Yuan does more to counter Chinese foreign influence in say Africa or belt and road programs than most anything else America could do.\n\n I'd say the Chinese are losing the most from the trade war, but no one is truly winning anything in this situation",
              "## upuprandom\n2. The ups and downs of the Huawei case. - so Huawei was banned from doing business with US companies, then it seemed that the ban would be reversed if the Chinese would buy more agricultural products, and now it seems that the ban is back on as China is no longer buying those agricultural products",
              "### comment › upuprandom\n> Chinese yuan is 15% weaker than in 2014, and at it's weakest since 2008. This is directly attributed to Trump's tariffs",
              "## Deleted User\nNeither. The whole affair is much ado about nothing - a political charade both governments are using to gain support, which in practice has changed very little. The best estimate for total GDP loss from China as a result of this war is 0.3%. Other estates report even lower losses. China’s foreign trade actually increased this year. The US, meanwhile, takes even less damage",
              "## caonim\nWe will come here today to analyze the progress of the Sino-US trade war in August 2019.\n\nSo far, Americans are levying a 25% tariff on goods worth about $250 billion in our country, plus $300 billion on September 1 is $550 billion",
              "We need to admit that the $250 billion that the United States has already imposed on China is harmful to us and has adverse effects. Of course, from the actual situation, this unfavorable result is jointly borne by China and the United States. Is the injury heavier?\n\nChina's manufacturing has shown strong competitiveness, under the influence of a 25% tariff of 250 billion US dollars,",
              "From January to July 2019, China’s exports to the United States were 1.62 trillion yuan, down 2.1%",
              "In other words, from January to July 2019, China’s exports to the United States only fell by more than 30 billion yuan, down only 2.1% (in RMB). Of course, due to the depreciation of the renminbi, in terms of the US dollar, the decline will be more, but not 10%.",
              "As we can see later in this article, some Chinese exports to the United States have been completed by bypassing other countries such as Vietnam. Therefore, in fact, exports to the United States in terms of renminbi can even be said to have not declined.\n\nBut we also want to see that tariffs do hurt China",
              "In the $250 billion commodity that has already imposed a 25% tariff, if you consider a 10% decline in exports in US dollars, there will be $225 billion a year, meaning that the year is more than $50 billion.\nSo who pays more than $50 billion in tariffs this year? In fact, Chinese companies and American consumers share the burden.",
              "Different reports have different opinions. According to the study of CIC's import of CIF prices from US Customs in January-May 2019, it is concluded that the approximate ratio of the two parties is about half to half, which means that each year is added. More than 50 billion US dollars of tariffs, Chinese companies bear more than 20 billion US dollars, American consumers bear more than 20 billion US dollars, typical mutual harm",
              "The amount of imports will also drop, and the total amount of tariffs that can be collected is more than 20 billion US dollars, and some of them will be borne by Chinese consumers. **Therefore, if we simply look at each other’s tariffs, the tariffs we impose on US companies are insufficient. To compensate for the loss of Chinese enterprises to pay export tariffs.**",
              "In order to cause equal harm to the other side, China certainly has a way.\n\nThat is to directly attack and eliminate US exports, causing greater losses to the United States.\n\nTo make up for China's gap in tariff collection.\n\nLet's look at the following set of data:\n\nFrom January to July 2019, the total trade volume between China and the United States was 2.1 trillion yuan, down 8.1%",

⚠️ [... middle content omitted — showing head and tail ...]

              "The new access to the supplier means that the funds invested in the Chinese suppliers have all been revoked, including the flight tickets and accommodation fees for engineers and designers to travel to China, and the cost of purchasing samples is not a small cost",
              "### caonim › caonim\nFlextronics publicly detained Huawei's goods worth hundreds of millions of yuan. FedEx publicly transferred Huawei's property to the United States, causing significant losses to Huawei. The list of unreliable entities in China has not been released, and it is also evaluating the impact and finding a suitable time.",
              "### caonim › caonim › caonim\nThis actually reduces the amount of manufacturing in China's domestic supply chain.\n\nOf course, Chinese companies are also spontaneously trying to evade the industrial chain.\n\nFor example, major domestic technology companies such as Huawei have clearly required suppliers to place factories in mainland China to ensure supply security, which is confirmed when many suppliers accept media reports.\n\nThe other is to export to the United States through other means"
